window.LMS_LOAD && window.LMS_LOAD("delivery-ownership", {"topics":[{"key":"delivery-ownership/production-debugging","sources":["David J. Agans, 2002 — Debugging: The 9 Indispensable Rules","Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering"],"why":"In development you control everything: the input, the code version, the data, and you can step through\nit. In production you get a ticket that says \"the assistant told a customer the wrong refund policy\nyesterday afternoon.\" The request is gone, the process that served it has been recycled, the vector index\nmay have been rebuilt since, and the model behind the API may have been updated by the provider.\n\nThe naive approach is to copy the user's question into a notebook, send it to the model, and see what\ncomes back. It almost always \"works\", and the ticket gets closed as \"cannot reproduce.\" That fails\nbecause the user's question is a small fraction of what the model actually received. The real input was a\nsystem prompt at some template version, plus chunks retrieved from the index *as it was then*, plus\nconversation history, plus tool outputs, sent to a specific model snapshot at a specific temperature.\nChange any of these and you are debugging a different request. On top of that, sampling makes the\nsystem stochastic: a failure that happens 1 time in 10 will look fixed in a single replay 90% of the\ntime.\n\nProduction debugging is the discipline of capturing enough at request time that the failure can be\nreconstructed afterwards, and of narrowing it down by evidence rather than by guessing and redeploying.","how":"### 1. Stabilize before you diagnose\nIf users are being harmed now, mitigation comes first: roll back the last prompt or model change, flip a\nfeature flag, route to a fallback, or disable the failing tool. You do this *before* understanding the\ncause because diagnosis can take hours while the damage accrues per request. Capture evidence (trace IDs,\na sample of bad outputs, current config) before rolling back, because the rollback destroys the live\nstate you would otherwise examine.\n\n### 2. Scope the failure\nAsk: since when, for whom, how often? Query metrics and logs to get a failure rate over time, sliced by\ntenant, model, prompt version, region and client version. A step change at 14:02 points at an event; a\nslow climb points at drift (index staleness, traffic mix). A failure confined to one tenant points at\ntheir data or config, not your code. Scoping turns \"it's broken\" into \"3% of tenant X's requests since\nthe 14:00 deploy.\"\n\n### 3. Find concrete failing requests\nPick specific failing requests via their correlation or trace ID, plus a few *succeeding* requests from\nthe same window. Comparing a bad trace against a good one (differential debugging) is usually faster than\nstudying the bad one alone, because the difference is the clue.\n\n### 4. Reconstruct the exact input\nFrom the trace, recover everything that determined the output:\n- the fully rendered messages, not the template plus variables;\n- the model identifier and snapshot the API actually reported, not the alias you configured;\n- temperature, top_p, max tokens, tool schemas, response format;\n- retrieved chunk IDs and their text *at that time*;\n- every tool call and its raw result, including errors and truncation.\nIf any of these was not logged, that gap is itself a finding: you will fix logging as an action item.\n\n### 5. Bisect the pipeline\nAn LLM application is a chain: input handling → retrieval → prompt assembly → model call → parsing →\ntool execution → post-processing. Check the intermediate output at each boundary. If the right document\nwas never retrieved, no prompt change will help; if it was retrieved but sat below a truncation limit,\nthe bug is in assembly; if it was in the prompt and the model ignored it, the bug is in generation.\nBisecting is logarithmic in the number of stages, which is why it beats reading the whole trace linearly.\n\n### 6. Replay with one variable changed, many times\nReplay the reconstructed input N times at the logged parameters to measure the failure rate, then change\nexactly one factor (current index instead of historical, new prompt version, other model) and measure\nagain. Repeating matters because a single stochastic sample proves nothing; changing one factor at a time\nmatters because changing two leaves you unable to say which one fixed it. Do not assume temperature 0\nmakes a hosted model deterministic: batching and floating-point effects on the provider side can still\nvary outputs, and seed parameters, where offered, are typically best-effort. So even a \"deterministic\"\nreplay deserves a handful of repeats before you trust it.\n\nStub side-effecting tools during replay. Feed the model the recorded tool results instead of calling the\nlive tools, *because* a replay that re-executes `issue_refund` is a second incident, and because live\ntools return today's data, which silently changes the input you were trying to hold fixed.\n\n### When the failure is latency, cost or errors rather than a wrong answer\nThe same scope-trace-bisect loop applies, but the evidence is the span waterfall rather than the content.\nFind the critical path in a slow trace: is the time in queueing before the model call, in the provider's\ntime to first token, in a long generation (output length often dominates end-to-end latency,\nbecause each output token is a separate decode step), in retries, or in a tool? Compare p50 against p99, because a\nhealthy median with a terrible tail usually means a minority of requests are doing something different\n(retry storms, oversized contexts, one slow tool, agent loops that ran many more steps), and the fix is\nto find what distinguishes them, not to speed up the average request. Cost spikes yield to the same\nslicing: tokens per request by tenant, route and prompt version usually reveal one path whose context or\nstep count grew.\n\n### 7. Correlate with change\nMost production regressions follow a change: a deploy, prompt edit, config or flag flip, index rebuild,\ndependency upgrade, upstream schema change, or a provider-side model update. A change log with timestamps\nlets you line up the onset of the failure against candidate causes.\n\n### 8. Close the loop\nThe fix ships with the captured failing case added to the regression suite, so the same bug cannot\nsilently return, and with any logging gaps found in step 4 closed.","example":"A minimal replay harness. It loads a logged trace, replays it at the original parameters to estimate the\nfailure rate, then swaps one factor. `complete` stands in for your provider client and `check` for a\ntask-specific assertion.\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom statistics import mean\n\ndef complete(messages, model, **params) -> str:\n    raise NotImplementedError(\"call your provider SDK here\")\n\ndef check(output: str) -> bool:\n    # task-specific: did the answer state the 30-day refund window?\n    return \"30 days\" in output\n\ndef replay(trace, n=20, **overrides):\n    t = {**trace, **overrides}            # override exactly one factor\n    outs = [complete(t[\"messages\"], t[\"model\"], **t[\"params\"]) for _ in range(n)]\n    return mean(not check(o) for o in outs)   # failure rate\n\ntrace = json.load(open(\"trace_7f3a.json\"))   # rendered messages, model, params, chunk ids\nbaseline = replay(trace)\nprint(f\"failure rate at logged inputs: {baseline:.0%}\")\n\n# Hypothesis: the retrieved chunk was the old policy page. Rebuild messages with today's chunk.\nfixed_ctx = dict(trace, messages=rebuild_with_current_chunks(trace))  # your helper\nprint(f\"with current index: {replay(fixed_ctx):.0%}\")\n\n# Hypothesis: temperature is the cause. Change only that.\nprint(f\"at temperature 0: {replay(trace, params={**trace['params'], 'temperature': 0}):.0%}\")\n```\n\nIf the baseline reproduces at, say, 40% and swapping in today's chunk drops it to 0% while temperature\n0 still fails, the defect is stale retrieval, not sampling, and the fix belongs in index freshness. Keep\nthe sample size honest: 0 failures in 20 replays only bounds the true rate below roughly 3/20 ≈ 15% at 95%\nconfidence (the \"rule of three\"), so a failure that was rare to begin with needs a few hundred replays\nbefore \"0%\" means fixed.","tradeoffs":["Logging full rendered prompts and tool results makes reconstruction possible, but costs storage and creates a PII store; it flips toward sampling or redaction when inputs are sensitive or volume is huge.","Rolling back immediately stops harm but destroys the live state you wanted to inspect; it is the right call whenever users are affected, provided you snapshot evidence first.","Replaying many samples gives a trustworthy failure rate but costs tokens and time; for deterministic failures (parsing, tool errors) a single replay is enough.","Debugging directly against production data gives the most faithful reproduction but risks side effects and access violations; prefer read-only replicas and replay with tools stubbed to recorded results."],"pitfalls":["Replaying only the user's question instead of the full rendered input; the symptom is a long run of \"cannot reproduce\" closures for problems customers keep reporting.","Trusting a single successful replay of a stochastic failure; the symptom is a fix that \"worked\" in testing and a failure rate in production that never moved.","Logging the configured model alias rather than the snapshot the API returned; the symptom is a regression nobody can explain because no deploy happened on your side.","Changing several things in one hotfix; the symptom is that the problem goes away and nobody can say why, so it returns on the next unrelated change.","Debugging before mitigating; the symptom is an hour of elegant diagnosis while the incident's impact keeps growing.","Replaying with live tools instead of recorded results; the symptom is duplicate side effects (a second email or refund) or replays whose tool outputs differ from the trace, so the \"reproduction\" is not one."],"when":"Use this practice whenever a live system produces wrong, slow or failed results and the cause is not\nobvious from one error message. The investment that pays for itself is upfront: trace IDs threaded\nthrough every stage, rendered prompts and retrieval results captured (or sampled), a change log, and a\nreplay harness. Measure your time-to-reproduce; if most investigations stall at \"cannot reproduce\", the\nproblem is instrumentation, not skill. In interviews, a strong answer to \"a customer says the bot gave a\nwrong answer\" walks through mitigate, scope, find the trace, reconstruct, bisect, replay and regress,\nrather than jumping to \"I'd tweak the prompt.\"","role_lens":{"engineer":"Instruments every stage with trace IDs, records rendered prompts and model snapshots, and owns the replay harness that turns tickets into reproducible test cases.","architect":"Decides what the platform must capture by default, how long it is retained, who can access it, and where redaction happens, so debugging is possible without a privacy breach.","fde":"Debugs inside a customer environment with limited access, so negotiates log export, read-only replicas and trace sharing early, before the first production issue arrives."},"check":{"q":"A customer reports the assistant cited a discontinued product twice last week. You paste the\nquestion into the playground and get a correct answer. What do you do next, and why is your playground\nresult not evidence?","a":"The playground used today's prompt template, today's index and possibly a different model\nsnapshot and temperature, so it tested a different request. I would find the two traces by request ID,\nrecover the rendered messages, retrieved chunk IDs and model snapshot, and check the retrieval stage\nfirst: was the discontinued product's page returned? If so, the index or its filters are stale and the\nfix is freshness, not the prompt. I would replay the logged input 20 or more times to get a failure rate,\nthen swap only the retrieved context to confirm the cause, and add the case to the regression suite."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should you replay a reconstructed LLM request many times rather than once?","options":["Providers rate-limit single requests more aggressively than batches","Sampling makes the output stochastic, so one replay cannot distinguish a fixed bug from a lucky draw","The first call warms the cache, so only later replays reflect production behaviour","Multiple replays are required to recover the original random seed"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With temperature above zero a failure may occur in only a fraction of samples; measuring the failure rate across many replays, before and after a change, is the only way to show the change mattered."},{"q":"An answer was wrong. The trace shows the correct document was the top retrieval result, but the prompt assembler's token budget cut it before the model call. Where is the defect?","options":["In the embedding model, which ranked the document too low","In the generation step, which should have inferred the missing content","In prompt assembly, which dropped retrieved content before it reached the model","In sampling, because a lower temperature would have found it"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Bisecting the pipeline shows retrieval did its job and the model never saw the content; the fix belongs in how context is budgeted and ordered, not in retrieval tuning or prompt wording."},{"q":"Users are receiving harmful outputs after a prompt change an hour ago. What comes first?","options":["Reproduce the failure locally so the rollback can be justified","Write the regression test so the bug cannot recur","Bisect the pipeline to locate the faulty stage","Capture sample trace IDs and config, then roll back the prompt change"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Mitigation precedes diagnosis when users are being harmed, but grabbing evidence first keeps the investigation possible after the rollback erases the live state."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/failure-analysis","sources":[],"why":"Production debugging explains one failure. But an AI product in production fails in hundreds of small\nways a day: an answer that misses a detail, a tool call with a wrong argument, a refusal that should\nnot have happened, a timeout. If the team reacts to each report as it arrives, effort goes to whatever\nis loudest: the angry enterprise customer, the screenshot on social media, the bug the CEO hit. The\nloudest failure is rarely the most common, and fixing it one at a time leaves the dominant failure modes\nuntouched.\n\nThe naive alternatives fail in specific ways. Reading the aggregate metric (\"satisfaction dropped from\n82% to 76%\") tells you something is wrong but not what to change. Swapping in a bigger model or rewriting\nthe whole prompt is a guess that costs money and often moves the error elsewhere. Writing an automated\nclassifier for failure types before anyone has looked at the data bakes in categories that the team\nimagined rather than the ones that occur.\n\nFailure analysis exists to turn a pile of individual failures into a ranked, quantified list of causes,\nso that the next fix is the one with the largest expected reduction in harm.","how":"### 1. Define what counts as a failure\nWrite down the signals: explicit (thumbs-down, escalation to a human, complaint), implicit (user\nrephrases the same question, abandons the session, copies nothing), system (exception, timeout, schema\nvalidation failure, tool error) and evaluation (an LLM judge or rule flags the output). Each source is\nbiased: thumbs-down over-represents engaged, annoyed users; system errors miss confident wrong answers.\nUsing several sources reduces the blind spots of any one.\n\n### 2. Sample without bias\nPull a random sample of failing cases from a fixed window, stratified if needed by tenant or use case,\nrather than the cases someone forwarded. A sample of a few hundred is usually enough to rank the top\nfailure modes, because you are estimating proportions, not finding every bug. Also pull some\n*successes*: they calibrate whether a \"failure pattern\" is really specific to failures.\n\nSeparately, label a small random sample of *all* traffic, flagged or not. Failure signals miss the\nconfident wrong answer that nobody thumbs down, so this sample is the only way to estimate the silent\nfailure rate, and it lets you turn \"38% of flagged failures\" into an absolute rate per conversation,\nwhich is what a fix's benefit is actually measured in.\n\n### 3. Open coding, then a taxonomy\nRead each case end to end (the trace, not just the final answer) and write a short free-text note on the\n*first* thing that went wrong. Anchoring on the first error matters because later errors are often\nconsequences of it: a wrong retrieval causes a wrong citation causes a wrong answer, and you want to count\none failure, not three. Keep reading until new cases stop producing new kinds of note (saturation);\nthat, not a fixed quota, tells you the taxonomy is complete enough to rank. After a pass, cluster the notes into categories (\"retrieved outdated policy\",\n\"tool called with wrong date format\", \"ignored instruction to ask for order ID\"). Each category is tagged\nwith the pipeline stage where it originated. Have a second person code a few dozen of the same cases:\nif the two of you disagree often, the category definitions are ambiguous and the counts that follow\nare noise. This is the same discipline the evals module calls error analysis; here it is applied to live\nproduction traffic.\n\n### 4. Count and weight\nCompute each category's share, with uncertainty: a proportion *p* from *n* samples has standard error\n√(p(1−p)/n). Then weight by severity, because a rare failure that leaks another tenant's data outranks a\ncommon one that adds an extra sentence. A simple score is frequency × severity × reach (fraction of users\nor revenue exposed).\n\n### 5. Attribute and choose fixes\nFor each top category, estimate the most a fix could buy before choosing one:\n\n```\nexpected reduction in failure rate ≈ overall failure rate × category share × fix efficacy\n```\n\nThe category share is a hard ceiling: a perfect fix for a 12% category removes at most 12% of failures,\nhowever impressive the intervention. Weighting that by severity and dividing by the fix's cost gives the ranking.\nThen ask what the cheapest effective intervention is at the stage that originated it:\nindex freshness for stale retrieval, a schema or validator for malformed tool arguments, a prompt rule or\nfew-shot example for ignored instructions, a guardrail for unsafe content. Fixing at the originating stage\nbeats patching downstream symptoms.\n\n### 6. Make it repeatable\nTurn the top categories into automated detectors or eval cases so their rate becomes a tracked metric,\nand repeat the manual pass periodically, because new features and traffic shifts create new categories.\n\n### Reactive versus proactive\nThis lesson is about analysing failures that happened. Its proactive counterpart, FMEA, enumerates\nfailure modes before launch and scores severity, occurrence and detectability; the two feed each other,\nsince observed failures update the FMEA's occurrence estimates.","example":"A support assistant handles about 10,000 conversations a week. The satisfaction metric has dropped and\nthe team's instinct is to switch to a larger model. Instead, they randomly sample 200 conversations that\nhad a failure signal and code the first error in each:\n\n| Category (originating stage)            | Count | Share |\n|-----------------------------------------|------:|------:|\n| Retrieved outdated policy (retrieval)   | 76    | 38%   |\n| Wrong order lookup argument (tool)      | 44    | 22%   |\n| Answered when it should escalate (policy)| 30   | 15%   |\n| Reasoning error with correct context (model) | 24 | 12% |\n| Timeout or provider error (infra)       | 16    | 8%    |\n| Other                                   | 10    | 5%    |\n\nThe top share has standard error √(0.38 × 0.62 / 200) ≈ 0.034, so a 95% interval of roughly 31%–45%:\nwide, but clearly the largest bucket. Only 12% of failures are genuine model reasoning errors, so a\nbigger model directly targets about an eighth of the problem while increasing cost on every call. It may\nhelp somewhat with tool arguments and escalation judgement, but it cannot fix stale retrieval or provider\ntimeouts, which together are 46% of failures.\nRe-indexing on document change plus filtering superseded policies attacks 38%; validating the order-ID\nformat before the tool call attacks another 22%. The escalation category is lower in frequency but higher\nin severity (a customer given a wrong commitment), so it goes second in priority despite its share.\n\nTo put the options in the same unit, suppose (illustratively) that 8% of conversations fail. A\nfreshness fix that removes 80% of stale-retrieval failures cuts the overall rate by about\n0.08 × 0.38 × 0.8 ≈ 2.4 percentage points. A larger model that fixed half of the reasoning errors would\ncut it by 0.08 × 0.12 × 0.5 ≈ 0.5 points, plus whatever spillover it has on tool arguments, at a higher\nprice on every request. The efficacy figures are guesses, which is fine: the point is that the\ncategory share bounds each option, and the next sample tells you whether the guess held.","tradeoffs":["Manual reading of traces finds categories nobody anticipated, but costs expert hours; it flips toward automated classifiers once the taxonomy is stable and volume makes reading every week impractical.","Larger samples narrow the confidence intervals, but ranking the top few modes rarely needs more than a few hundred cases; spend extra effort on rare, high-severity categories instead.","A fine-grained taxonomy gives precise fixes but sparse counts per category; merge categories when most have only a handful of cases.","Coding only the first error keeps counts honest, but hides compounding issues; record secondary errors separately when you suspect two independent defects."],"pitfalls":["Analysing only the failures customers forwarded; the symptom is a priority list that tracks your largest accounts rather than your most common defects.","Reading only final answers instead of full traces; the symptom is that almost everything is labelled \"model hallucinated\" and no stage-specific fix emerges.","Counting cascaded errors as separate failures; the symptom is categories whose totals exceed the number of cases and fixes that all point at the same upstream defect.","Building the automated failure classifier before doing a manual pass; the symptom is a large \"other\" bucket and categories that never change week to week.","Ranking by frequency alone; the symptom is the team polishing tone while a rare data-exposure or wrong-commitment failure keeps recurring.","Sampling only flagged conversations; the symptom is a reported failure rate that looks stable while customers, on manual review, turn out to be receiving confidently wrong answers nobody flagged."],"when":"Run a failure-analysis pass when an aggregate quality metric moves, before choosing between expensive\ninterventions (model swap, fine-tuning, re-architecture), at the end of a pilot, and on a regular cadence\nfor any system in production. Do not use it as a substitute for incident response: a single severe\nfailure needs debugging and mitigation now, not a sampling exercise. Measure category shares over time;\na fix is confirmed when its category's share falls in the next sample. In interviews, answering \"quality\ndropped, what do you do?\" with \"sample failures, categorise by stage, rank by impact\" signals maturity.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Pulls the sample, codes traces, and converts the top failure categories into automated detectors and regression eval cases that run on every change.","architect":"Uses the stage attribution to decide where to invest, for example retrieval freshness infrastructure versus model upgrades, and ensures traces exist to make analysis possible.","fde":"Runs the analysis with the customer's subject-matter experts, whose judgement of what counts as a failure and how severe it is often differs from the vendor's assumptions."},"check":{"q":"Leadership wants to fine-tune a model because \"accuracy is 78%\". How do you decide whether\nfine-tuning is the right investment?","a":"I would sample a few hundred failing cases at random, read the full traces and code the first error\nwith its originating stage. Fine-tuning mainly addresses failures where the model had the right context and\nstill answered wrongly, or where learned behaviour is the issue (output format, tool-argument conventions,\nwhen to escalate). If most failures originate in retrieval, data freshness or infrastructure, fine-tuning\ncannot fix them, and even model-behaviour failures are often cheaper to fix with validators or prompt rules,\nso the money is better spent at those stages. I would show the category shares with confidence intervals, weight them by severity, and\nestimate the ceiling each intervention could reach before committing."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should failure coding anchor on the first thing that went wrong in a trace?","options":["The first error is always the most severe one","Later errors are often consequences of the first, so counting them separately inflates downstream categories","Models only make one error per conversation","It makes the analysis faster because the rest of the trace can be skipped"],"answer":1,"explanation":"A wrong retrieval often causes a wrong citation and then a wrong answer; attributing all three to the originating defect keeps counts honest and points the fix at the stage that actually caused the failure."},{"q":"Which failure sample is the least biased basis for prioritisation?","options":["All conversations escalated by the five largest customers","Every thumbs-down from the past day","A random sample from all failure signals over a fixed window, plus a few successes for comparison","The cases the support team flagged as most embarrassing"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Forwarded or explicit-feedback samples over-represent loud accounts and engaged users; random sampling across several signal types estimates true proportions, and successes show whether a pattern is failure-specific."},{"q":"In a sample of 200 failures, 12% are model reasoning errors with correct context. What does that imply about switching to a stronger model?","options":["It will fix most failures because stronger models also retrieve better","It is pointless because 12% is within the margin of error","It directly targets roughly that share and cannot fix retrieval or infra failures, so other stages likely offer larger gains","It should be done first because model errors are the hardest to fix otherwise"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A stronger model improves generation, not stale indexes or provider timeouts; its main lever is the share of failures that originate in the model (plus some spillover into tool arguments and instruction following), so larger categories elsewhere usually deserve priority."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/incident-severity-and-escalation","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer, Murphy, Rensin, Kawahara, Thorne (eds.), 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook"],"why":"Without agreed severity levels, every incident starts with a negotiation. One engineer thinks a 5% error\nrate is a shrug; another pages the VP. Small issues wake people at 3 a.m. and burn out on-call, while\nserious ones sit in a ticket queue because the person who noticed did not feel entitled to raise the\nalarm. Customers get updates erratically, and the contractual clock in an SLA runs while people debate.\n\nThe naive alternative, \"use judgement\", fails under exactly the conditions incidents create: stress,\npartial information, and someone senior asking whether it is really that bad. Classifying by cause\n(\"it's just a config bug\") fails too, because cause is unknown at the start and the same root cause can\nbe trivial or catastrophic depending on how many users it reaches.\n\nAI products add a twist. A conventional outage is loud: errors spike, health checks fail. An AI\nincident is often silent: the service returns HTTP 200 with fluent, wrong, or unsafe content. If severity\ncriteria only mention availability and latency, a model that starts giving incorrect medical dosage\ninformation, or leaking one tenant's documents into another's answers, may not register as an incident\nat all.","how":"### Severity is defined by impact\nA typical scale has four or five levels. The exact criteria are organisation-specific; a common shape:\n- **SEV1 / critical:** broad user-facing outage or degradation of a core function, data exposure or\n  security breach, safety harm, or legal or regulatory exposure. All hands, leadership informed.\n- **SEV2 / major:** significant degradation for a meaningful share of users or a key customer, with no\n  workaround. Urgent, paged response.\n- **SEV3 / minor:** limited impact or a workaround exists. Handled in working hours by the owning team.\n- **SEV4 / low:** cosmetic or internal-only. Goes to the backlog.\nCriteria are written as observable facts (fraction of requests or tenants affected, whether data crossed\na tenant boundary, whether a contractual SLA is at risk) *because* responders must classify within\nminutes, with incomplete information.\n\n### AI-specific criteria\nAdd impact criteria that are about content, not uptime: unsafe or harmful outputs reaching users; cross-\ntenant or PII leakage, including through prompt injection; an agent taking unauthorised or irreversible\nactions; a measured quality metric (groundedness, task success) falling past a defined threshold;\na provider model change or outage affecting all traffic. A single confirmed data-exposure case can\njustify the top severity even if it touched one user, because the harm is not proportional to volume.\n\n### Declare early, adjust freely\nTeams are told to declare at the higher plausible severity and downgrade as facts arrive, *because*\nunder-declaring delays response while over-declaring costs only some attention. Severity is not a verdict\non anyone; it is a routing decision and should change as the picture changes. The asymmetry applies to a\nhuman declaring a suspected incident, not to automated alert thresholds: a page that fires on noise has a\nreal cost, because people learn to ignore it, so alerts should be tuned for precision while declaration\nstays biased high.\n\n### Severity is not priority\nSeverity measures impact; priority is the order in which work gets done, and it also weighs urgency\n(is the harm growing or stable?) and available effort. A SEV3 with a workaround can still be top of the\nqueue because the workaround expires tomorrow. Customer contracts usually define their own ticket\npriorities (P1 to P4 or similar) with response targets, so the policy must map them explicitly to\ninternal severities; otherwise a customer's P1 lands as an internal SEV3 and the SLA clock runs out\nwhile both sides think the other is on it. A related line to draw: a defect becomes an *incident* when it\nneeds a coordinated response now, not when it is merely a bug in the backlog.\n\n### Severity drives the response\nEach level maps to: who is paged, a target time to acknowledge and to mitigate, whether an incident\ncommander is assigned, the update cadence for stakeholders and customers, and whether a postmortem is\nmandatory. The commander coordinates and decides; others investigate, communicate, or scribe. Splitting\nthese roles stops the person debugging from also fielding questions from executives.\n\n### Two kinds of escalation\n- **Functional escalation** brings in expertise or ownership: the retrieval team, the platform on-call,\n  the model provider's support with request IDs and timestamps. Trigger: the current responders cannot\n  make progress or the fault is in someone else's component.\n- **Hierarchical escalation** brings in authority: a decision to take a feature offline, notify customers\n  or regulators, spend money, or override a customer's change freeze. Trigger: the needed decision is above\n  the responder's remit.\nBoth should also be **time-based**: if a SEV2 is not mitigated within the policy's window, it escalates\nautomatically, so no one has to judge whether asking for help is admitting failure.","example":"An illustrative policy (the targets are examples, not a standard) and a timeline for an enterprise RAG\nassistant.\n\nPolicy excerpt: SEV2 = a key customer or more than 5% of requests degraded with no workaround; page\non-call, acknowledge within 15 minutes, updates every 30 minutes, escalate to engineering manager if not\nmitigated in 60 minutes. Any confirmed cross-tenant data exposure = SEV1.\n\n- **09:10** Alert: 4% of responses fail JSON schema validation (later traced to a provider model alias\n  moving to a newer snapshot). Failures fall back to a generic answer. On-call declares SEV3: workaround exists, small share.\n- **09:25** Scoping shows all failures are from one enterprise tenant whose prompts use a tool schema the\n  new snapshot handles differently; for them, 60% of requests fail. That tenant is a named account with an\n  SLA. Upgraded to SEV2; incident commander assigned; account team informed.\n- **09:40** Functional escalation: ticket to the provider with ten request IDs. Mitigation: pin that\n  tenant to the previous model snapshot.\n- **09:55** Failure rate back to baseline. Customer update sent. Incident stays open at SEV3 until the\n  schema is made compatible with the new snapshot.\n- **10:30** During review, an engineer notices one fallback answer contained a document title from a\n  different tenant's cache. Re-declared SEV1 immediately: security on-call paged, leadership and legal\n  informed, cache disabled.\n\nThe severity changed three times (SEV3, SEV2, SEV3, SEV1), each time because new facts about impact arrived, not because the cause\nchanged.","tradeoffs":["Low thresholds catch real incidents early but page people for noise; they flip toward higher thresholds when on-call fatigue causes alerts to be ignored, which is worse than a slower page.","Detailed severity criteria reduce argument but take time to apply under pressure; keep the top levels simple and observable, and put nuance in the lower levels.","Automatic time-based escalation removes social friction but can pull managers into incidents that are nearly resolved; allow the commander to acknowledge and defer with a stated reason.","Treating quality degradations as incidents protects users but requires a trustworthy online quality metric; without one, quality incidents generate false alarms and are quietly ignored."],"pitfalls":["Defining severity only by uptime and error rate; the symptom is a harmful or wrong-answer episode that ran for days because every dashboard stayed green.","Classifying by suspected cause rather than impact; the symptom is \"it's only a config change\" incidents that turn out to affect every tenant.","Reluctance to declare or escalate for fear of blame; the symptom is incidents first heard about from customers or account managers rather than from monitoring or on-call.","One person acting as commander, debugger and communicator; the symptom is customer updates that stop for an hour exactly when the incident is worst.","Escalating to a model provider without request IDs, timestamps and a minimal reproduction; the symptom is a support ticket that sits unanswered while you wait.","Severity inflation, where everything urgent-sounding is declared SEV1 and never downgraded; the symptom is SEV1 pages that no longer mobilise anyone and a postmortem backlog nobody can clear."],"when":"Every team running a production service needs a written severity scale and escalation policy before the\nfirst incident, agreed with support, account management and, for enterprise deals, aligned with the SLA's\npriority definitions. Revisit it after each postmortem that revealed a classification dispute. For AI\nsystems, add content-level criteria and the online signals that detect them. Measure time to declare,\ntime to acknowledge and time to mitigate by severity, and how often severity changed during incidents.\nIn interviews, expect \"how would you classify a hallucination that reached one customer?\" and answer by\nimpact and harm, not by error rate.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Writes alerts that map to severity criteria, carries the pager, and knows how to pin a model snapshot or disable a feature as the first mitigation.","architect":"Defines the severity scale with AI-specific impact criteria and ensures the system has kill switches and per-tenant controls that make fast mitigation possible.","fde":"Aligns the customer's own severity scale and contacts with the vendor's, so an incident in the customer's environment escalates to the right people on both sides within minutes."},"check":{"q":"One user reports that the assistant quoted a paragraph from a document they should not have access\nto. Error rates and latency are normal. What severity do you assign and what happens next?","a":"Top severity, provisionally. Impact, not volume, drives classification: a single confirmed access-\ncontrol breach is a data exposure with legal and contractual consequences, and it may affect many users\nwho simply did not notice. I would page security and the owning team, assign a commander, preserve the\ntrace, and mitigate fast, for example by disabling the retrieval path or the cache involved. Hierarchical\nescalation covers customer and possibly regulatory notification. If investigation shows the user did\nhave access, we downgrade; declaring high and downgrading is cheaper than the reverse."},"quiz":[{"q":"What should primarily determine an incident's severity level?","options":["How difficult the root cause is expected to be to fix","Which team's component is suspected to be at fault","The observed impact on users, data, safety and contractual obligations","How long the system has been in production"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Cause and ownership are usually unknown when an incident starts, and the same cause can be trivial or severe; impact is observable early and determines how urgent and broad the response must be."},{"q":"What distinguishes hierarchical escalation from functional escalation?","options":["Hierarchical escalation is automatic, functional escalation is manual","Hierarchical brings in authority to make decisions; functional brings in expertise or the owning team","Hierarchical escalation is only used for security incidents","Functional escalation always goes to an external vendor"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Functional escalation adds people who can diagnose or fix the component; hierarchical escalation adds people who can approve decisions such as taking a feature offline or notifying customers."},{"q":"Why do incident policies tell responders to declare at the higher plausible severity?","options":["Higher severity incidents are exempt from postmortems","It guarantees the SLA clock stops running","Customers expect every incident to be reported as critical","Under-declaring delays the response while over-declaring only costs attention, and severity can be lowered later"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The costs are asymmetric: a late response to a real SEV1 compounds harm, whereas downgrading after facts arrive is cheap, so the policy biases toward early, high declaration."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/root-cause-analysis-and-postmortems","sources":["Beyer, Jones, Petoff, Murphy (eds.), 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Richard I. Cook, 1998 — How Complex Systems Fail","John Allspaw, 2012 — Blameless PostMortems and a Just Culture"],"why":"Mitigation ends the incident; it does not stop the next one. If the team rolls back, closes the ticket\nand moves on, the same class of failure returns in a different shape, because whatever let it through\n(a missing test, a silent alert, a deploy path without review) is still there.\n\nTwo naive alternatives are common. The first is to stop at the proximate cause: \"a bad prompt change\nwas deployed.\" That names the trigger and suggests \"be more careful\", which is not an action anyone can\nexecute. The second is to find someone to blame: \"the engineer skipped the eval.\" Blame feels like\naccountability but produces the opposite: people learn to hide mistakes and sanitise timelines, so the\norganisation loses the very information it needs. It also misreads how failures happen. In complex\nsystems, incidents typically need several latent conditions to line up, and the human action at the end\nwas usually reasonable given what that person could see at the time.\n\nFor AI systems the temptation to stop early is stronger, because \"the model is non-deterministic\" seems\nlike an explanation. It is not one: a well-built system expects wrong model outputs and has grounding\nchecks, validation, guardrails and human review sized to the risk. The question is why none of those\ncaught this one.","how":"### 1. Build the timeline first\nAssemble a factual, timestamped timeline from logs, alerts, chat and deploy records: when the trigger\nhappened, when impact began, when it was detected and by whom, each decision and its rationale, when it\nwas mitigated and resolved. Doing this before analysis matters *because* memory is reconstructive; the\ntimeline anchors discussion in evidence and exposes gaps such as a 40-minute stretch between impact and\ndetection.\n\n### 2. Ask \"why\" along several chains\nThe **five whys** technique repeatedly asks why the previous answer happened. It is useful for getting\npast the trigger, but it produces a single linear chain and stops wherever the asker's knowledge or\npatience runs out. So run it along several axes: why did the defect exist, why did it reach production,\nwhy was it not detected sooner, why did mitigation take as long as it did. An **Ishikawa (fishbone)**\ndiagram groups candidate causes by category (code, data, model, process, people, environment) and helps\navoid tunnel vision. A **fault tree** works backwards from the failure through AND/OR gates and is\nuseful when you need to show that several conditions were jointly necessary.\n\n### 3. Prefer contributing factors over \"the root cause\"\nWrite down every condition without which the incident would not have happened or would have been\nsmaller. This framing is more accurate for complex systems and produces better actions, because each\nfactor is a separate place to add a defence. It is the \"Swiss cheese\" picture: each layer of defence has\nholes, and an incident is the rare moment they line up, so shrinking the holes in any one layer helps.\n\nTest each candidate factor in two ways. The counterfactual test: had this condition been absent, would\nthe incident have been prevented or smaller? If not, it is background, not a factor. The evidence test:\nis the causal link shown by logs, traces or a reproduction, or only plausible? A plausible story that\nnobody checked is how postmortems confidently fix the wrong thing.\n\n### When to stop asking why\nStop a chain when you reach a condition that a team can change with a system control and own as an\naction. Going one step further usually lands on something true but useless (\"people make mistakes\",\n\"the deadline was tight\"); going one step short leaves a trigger (\"a bad config was pushed\"). If the\nchain ends in an organisational factor such as no budget for eval coverage, record it and escalate it as\na structural finding rather than pretending a ticket can close it.\n\n### 4. Stay blameless, not accountability-free\nDescribe actions in terms of the information and pressures people had: \"the eval suite passed because it\ncontained no cases for policy documents\" rather than \"Sam didn't test properly.\" The goal is a safe\nenvironment for honest accounts; accountability lives in owning the action items.\n\n### 5. Write the postmortem\nA standard structure: summary; impact (users, duration, data, money, SLA); timeline; detection (how and\nhow fast); response; contributing factors; what went well; where we got lucky; action items. \"Where we got\nlucky\" captures near-misses that would otherwise be forgotten.\n\n### 6. Action items that actually close\nEach action has a single owner, a due date, a tracking ticket and a category: **prevent** (stop the\ndefect existing), **detect** (catch it faster), **mitigate** (limit blast radius or speed recovery).\nPrefer actions that change the system (a gate, a test, an alert, a kill switch) over ones that rely on\npeople remembering. Review open postmortem actions on a fixed cadence; an action with no owner or date is\na wish.\n\n### 7. Share and learn\nPublish postmortems internally and look across them periodically; recurring factors (no eval coverage for\nnew document types, provider changes going unnoticed) are where structural investment pays off.","example":"Incident: for six hours an HR assistant told employees the parental-leave policy was 12 weeks; the\ncurrent policy is 16. Proximate trigger: a re-indexing job ingested an archived policy folder.\n\nFive whys along the *defect* chain: the wrong answer was generated because the old PDF ranked top;\nit ranked top because both versions were indexed with no effective-date metadata; there was no metadata\nbecause the ingestion connector was configured by folder, and the archive sat inside the indexed folder;\nnobody noticed because the connector config was not code-reviewed.\n\nAlong the *detection* chain: the issue was found by an employee complaint, not monitoring, because the\ngroundedness check verified that answers matched retrieved text (they did) rather than that the source\nwas current; there were no eval cases asserting current policy values.\n\nContributing factors: archive inside an indexed path; no effective-date filter; unreviewed connector\nconfig; eval suite with no freshness cases; detection reliant on users.\n\nAction items:\n- Prevent: index only documents with an effective date and filter superseded versions (owner: retrieval team, 2 weeks).\n- Prevent: connector configuration in version control with review (owner: platform, 3 weeks).\n- Detect: run 30 \"current value\" questions from HR after every re-index and nightly, since a nightly-only check could be slower than the six hours it took a user to complain (owner: AI engineer and HR SME, 2 weeks).\n- Mitigate: per-collection kill switch to exclude a source within minutes (owner: platform, 4 weeks).\n\nNote what is absent: \"the model hallucinated\" and \"the admin should have checked the folder.\"","tradeoffs":["Deep analysis of every incident yields learning but consumes senior time; reserve full postmortems for higher severities and near-misses with high potential impact, and use a short template for the rest.","The \"contributing factors\" framing is more accurate than a single root cause, but can produce a long list with no priority; rank actions by how many future failure paths each one closes.","Publishing postmortems widely spreads lessons, but candid detail can be sensitive for customers; share an external version with impact and corrective actions, and keep the internal one fully candid.","Systemic fixes (gates, tests, automation) are durable but slower to ship than process reminders; flip toward a temporary process control only as a bridge, with a dated plan for the systemic fix."],"pitfalls":["Stopping at the trigger (\"bad deploy\", \"model hallucinated\"); the symptom is action items like \"be more careful\" and the same class of incident recurring within months.","Blame, explicit or implied; the symptom is vague timelines, defensive meetings and fewer incidents reported even as customer complaints rise.","Action items without owners, dates or tickets; the symptom is a postmortem archive whose actions are mostly still open when the next similar incident happens.","Only \"prevent\" actions; the symptom is that a different trigger produces the same long, user-detected outage because detection and mitigation never improved.","Writing the postmortem weeks later; the symptom is a timeline reconstructed from memory with gaps exactly where the important decisions were made.","Hindsight bias, judging decisions by an outcome nobody could see at the time; the symptom is \"should have\" and \"failed to\" language in the timeline, and no explanation of why the action looked reasonable to the person who took it."],"when":"Write a postmortem for every incident above a severity threshold defined in your incident policy, for\nany data exposure or safety event regardless of scale, and for near-misses that would have been severe.\nStart the timeline while the incident is fresh and hold the review within days. Measure action-item\ncompletion rate and how often incidents share contributing factors with earlier ones; both tell you\nwhether postmortems are changing anything. In interviews, a strong answer separates trigger, contributing\nfactors and detection gaps, and proposes system changes rather than personal vigilance.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the timeline from traces and deploy logs, and turns detection gaps into concrete eval cases, alerts and regression tests with owners.","architect":"Looks across postmortems for recurring structural factors and funds the platform changes, such as release gates or source freshness controls, that close whole classes of failure.","fde":"Writes the customer-facing version, joins joint reviews with the customer's teams, and makes sure factors on the customer's side become actions they own."},"check":{"q":"A postmortem draft says: \"Root cause: the LLM hallucinated a refund amount. Action: improve the\nprompt.\" What is wrong with it, and how would you rewrite the analysis?","a":"Hallucination is an expected behaviour of the component, so it is a symptom, not a cause; the\nquestion is why the system let an unverified amount reach a customer. I would ask why the amount was\ngenerated rather than looked up from the order system, why no validator compared it to the order record,\nwhy evals did not cover refund amounts, and how the issue was detected. Actions would then be: fetch\namounts via a tool, validate before sending, add eval cases, and alert on mismatches, each with an owner\nand date. \"Improve the prompt\" may stay, but only as one item among them."},"quiz":[{"q":"What is the main limitation of the five-whys technique in complex systems?","options":["It requires specialised software to run","It follows one linear chain and stops where the asker's knowledge ends, missing parallel contributing factors","It can only be applied to hardware failures","It always assigns the cause to an individual"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Incidents usually need several conditions at once; a single chain of whys finds one of them. Running it along prevention, detection and mitigation axes, or using a fishbone or fault tree, surfaces the rest."},{"q":"Why are blameless postmortems more effective than assigning individual fault?","options":["They remove the need for anyone to own follow-up actions","They shorten the postmortem meeting","People give honest, detailed accounts when they are not punished, which reveals the system conditions behind the error","Regulators require that individuals never be named"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Blame drives people to hide or sanitise what happened, destroying the information needed to fix the system; blamelessness keeps accounts honest while accountability lives in owned action items."},{"q":"Which action item best addresses a detection gap in an AI incident found by a user complaint?","options":["Remind the team to review outputs more carefully during releases","Switch to a more capable model","Add a disclaimer that answers may be inaccurate","Add an automated eval or online check for the failure type, with an owner and due date"],"answer":3,"explanation":"A detection action should catch the failure automatically before users do; reminders and disclaimers do not detect anything, and a model swap is a prevention guess rather than a detection control."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/operational-ownership-and-support-boundaries","sources":[],"why":"A deployed AI system is a stack of things owned by different people: the model provider's API, your\nplatform and serving layer, the application's prompts, retrieval and tools, the customer's identity\nprovider, network and data sources, and often a customer-built front end. When an answer is wrong or a\nrequest fails, the cause can be in any layer, and each owner's first instinct is to show it is not\ntheirs. The customer, meanwhile, just sees \"your AI is broken.\"\n\nThe naive arrangement is implicit ownership: whoever built it, or whoever is most helpful, fixes it. In\nforward-deployed work this usually means the engineer who did the integration keeps getting called months\nafter go-live, for problems in systems they no longer control. It does not scale, it makes that person a\nsingle point of failure, and it hides the true cost of supporting the deployment. The opposite failure is\ntight \"not our problem\" boundaries with no path across them, where a ticket about stale answers is closed\nbecause the stale documents live in the customer's SharePoint.\n\nExplicit ownership and boundaries exist so that every component has someone who will act, every party\nknows what it must do, and issues that span boundaries have a defined route instead of a debate.","how":"### Owners have pager, access and authority\nA team owns a component only if it carries three things together: it is paged when it fails, it has the\naccess needed to diagnose and change it, and it has the authority (and budget) to prioritise fixes.\nOwnership without access produces helpless on-call; access without ownership produces drive-by changes no\none is accountable for. A service catalog records each component's owner, on-call rotation, runbook,\ndependencies and support hours.\n\n### Draw the boundary by layer\nFor each layer of the stack, state who is responsible for operating it, who for changing it, and who for\nits inputs. A shared-responsibility table is the usual artefact, sometimes expressed as RACI (Responsible,\nAccountable, Consulted, Informed), *because* \"who must act\" and \"who must be told\" are different\nquestions and conflating them causes either paralysis or surprise. The rule that makes RACI useful is\nexactly one Accountable per row: several Responsible parties can share the work, but if two parties are\nAccountable, each can wait for the other, and a row with none is unowned by construction.\n\n### Supported configurations and coverage\nA boundary is also a statement of *what* you support and *when*. A supported-configuration list names the\nversions, models, connectors and deployment shapes you will diagnose; a customer who swaps in their own\nmodel or edits the system prompt has moved outside it, and the agreement should say whether support then\nbecomes best-effort. Coverage hours matter as much as scope: a 24×7 SEV1 commitment needs a staffed\nrotation (or follow-the-sun across time zones), while business-hours support must say what happens to a\nSEV1 at 02:00 on Saturday. Mismatched hours between vendor and customer are a classic gap, because\neach side's on-call assumes the other's is awake.\n\n### Define the crossing points\nMost real incidents span a boundary, so the important part is the interface: what evidence the reporting\nparty must provide (request IDs, timestamps, tenant), which channel to use, response targets by severity,\nand who leads joint incidents. \"Out of scope\" should mean \"we route it to the right owner with\nevidence\", not \"we close the ticket.\"\n\n### Tiered support\nSupport is usually layered: tier 1 takes the report, gathers evidence and resolves known issues from\nrunbooks; tier 2 diagnoses using logs and tooling; tier 3 is the engineering team that can change code,\nprompts or models. Each tier needs documentation to resolve what it can, *because* every issue that\nreaches engineering unnecessarily is expensive and slows real fixes. The useful measures are the share\nof tickets resolved at each tier and the share escalated with complete evidence: a tier 3 that keeps\nasking \"which request ID?\" is doing tier 1's job.\n\n### Plan the handover\nDeployments move through phases: build, launch, a period of elevated attention (hypercare) and steady\nstate. Ownership should change at defined points with exit criteria, such as a stable error and quality\nbaseline, runbooks validated by the receiving team, and alerts routed to their on-call. Without this, the\nbuild team never actually lets go.\n\n### AI-specific boundaries\nMake explicit the things that are new in AI systems: who owns answer quality (the prompt and eval owner,\nnot the platform), who owns source data freshness and access permissions (often the customer), who decides\nwhen a provider model snapshot changes, and who responds to harmful-output reports. These are the\nquestions most likely to be unowned by default.","example":"A vendor deploys a RAG assistant into a bank's private cloud. The boundary table agreed at handover:\n\n| Layer | Operates | Changes | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Model endpoint (cloud provider) | Provider | Vendor chooses snapshot | Vendor escalates to provider |\n| Application, prompts, retrieval, evals | Vendor | Vendor | Quality incidents are the vendor's |\n| Kubernetes cluster, network, SSO | Bank platform team | Bank | Vendor has read-only logs |\n| Source document repositories | Bank content owners | Bank | Freshness and permissions are the bank's |\n| Chat front end in the intranet | Bank web team | Bank | Vendor supplies API only |\n\nWeek 9 ticket: \"answers cite last year's expense policy.\" Tier 1 at the bank collects three request IDs.\nVendor tier 3 traces them: retrieval returned the 2025 document because the 2026 one was uploaded to a\nfolder not included in the connector scope. The cause is on the bank's side (document placement), the\ndetection gap is on the vendor's (no freshness eval). Resolution: the bank's content owner moves the\nfile and adopts a publishing checklist; the vendor adds a freshness eval and an alert when a source's\nnewest document is older than a set threshold. The ticket is resolved in a day because both sides knew\nwho did what, and neither closed it as \"not ours\".","tradeoffs":["\"You build it, you run it\" aligns incentives and shortens feedback, but loads product teams with on-call; it flips toward a dedicated operations or platform team when many small services share infrastructure.","Narrow support boundaries keep costs predictable, but frustrate customers when problems straddle them; widen them, or sell an extended tier, for strategic accounts where retention matters more than margin.","Keeping FDEs as the first line after launch gives customers expert help, but stops FDEs from moving to new deployments; it only works for a time-boxed hypercare window with a dated handover.","Granting the vendor broad access inside a customer environment speeds diagnosis, but raises security and compliance burden; read-only, audited access to logs and traces is usually the workable middle."],"pitfalls":["Ownership assigned to a team without access or authority; the symptom is on-call that can only open tickets for someone else and mean time to mitigate measured in days.","Orphaned components after a reorganisation or project end; the symptom is alerts routed to a channel nobody reads and a service catalog entry naming someone who has left.","No owner for answer quality; the symptom is a slow decline in user satisfaction with every team pointing at another layer and no one running evals.","Handover without exit criteria; the symptom is the original builders still being paged months after go-live, often unofficially through direct messages.","Boundaries written only in the contract; the symptom is support staff on both sides who have never seen the table and argue scope during an incident.","Two parties marked Accountable for the same layer, often \"shared\" data quality; the symptom is a known problem that stays open for weeks while each side waits for the other to schedule the fix."],"when":"Define ownership and boundaries before go-live of any production system, and revisit them at each phase\nchange, reorganisation or new integration. For multi-party AI deployments, agree the boundary table with\nthe customer as part of deployment readiness, mirror it in the SLA, runbooks and support tooling, and\ntest it with a tabletop exercise. Measure tickets bounced between parties, time to route an issue to its\nowner, and the share of tickets reaching engineering that tier 1 or 2 could have resolved. In interviews,\nexpect \"after launch, who supports this?\" and answer with owners, crossing points and a handover plan.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the runbooks, dashboards and evidence-gathering tools that let other tiers resolve issues without escalating to the engineers who wrote the code.","architect":"Draws component boundaries so each has one owner, and designs the access model and interfaces that let ownership cross organisations safely.","fde":"Negotiates the boundary table with the customer, runs hypercare, and executes the handover so the deployment does not depend on them personally after it ends."},"check":{"q":"Six months after launch, the FDE who built a customer's deployment still receives most of its\nsupport requests by direct message. What is wrong, and what would you change?","a":"Ownership was never transferred: the deployment has an implicit owner with no on-call rotation,\nno shared runbooks and no defined route for the customer's issues. That makes one person a single point\nof failure and hides the support cost. I would write the boundary table with the customer, register the\ncomponents in the service catalog with an owning team and rotation, route requests to a support queue\nwith tiering, make sure runbooks and dashboards let tier 2 diagnose common issues, and set handover exit\ncriteria the receiving team signs off. The FDE then becomes a tier 3 escalation, not the front door."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which combination makes a team a genuine operational owner of a component?","options":["It wrote the original code and is listed in the README","It is paged for failures, has access to diagnose and change it, and has authority to prioritise fixes","It has admin credentials to the production environment","It attends the weekly status meeting with the customer"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Paging without access, or access without accountability, both fail; ownership requires being alerted, being able to act, and being empowered to choose the work."},{"q":"A user reports stale answers; the stale document lives in the customer's repository, outside your support scope. What is the correct response?","options":["Close the ticket as out of scope and inform the user","Silently fix the repository yourself using any access you have","Route it to the customer's content owner with evidence, and check whether your side should have detected it","Retrain the embedding model on the latest documents"],"answer":2,"explanation":"A boundary defines who acts, not whether the issue matters; out-of-scope issues are routed with evidence, and detection gaps on your side remain your action items."},{"q":"What is the main purpose of exit criteria for a hypercare period?","options":["To decide when the customer can stop paying for support","To guarantee no incidents happen after handover","To let the build team skip writing runbooks","To make the transfer of ownership a deliberate, verified step rather than something that never happens"],"answer":3,"explanation":"Without criteria such as a stable baseline, validated runbooks and alerts routed to the new owner, builders stay informally on the hook indefinitely and the receiving team is never ready."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/slas","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Beyer et al., 2018 — The Site Reliability Workbook"],"why":"An enterprise buyer is about to route a business process through your AI system. Procurement, legal\nand their own operations team need to know what happens when it is down at 2 a.m., how fast someone\nanswers, and what they are owed if it keeps happening. Without a written SLA, expectations are set\nimplicitly: by the best week of the pilot, by what a salesperson said on a call, or by the customer's\nexperience with a hyperscaler. Every outage then becomes an argument about whether it \"counted\".\n\nThe naive approaches fail in predictable ways. Copying a cloud provider's headline number ignores that\nyour service sits on top of several dependencies, each of which can fail independently, so your honest\nceiling is lower than any one of theirs. Promising what the pilot achieved ignores that a pilot ran at a\ntenth of production traffic with the builders watching it. Promising \"24/7 support\" without a rotation\nbehind it converts the first night incident into a breach. And promising answer quality as if it were\nuptime creates a metric neither side can measure the same way.\n\nAn SLA also changes what an incident is. Once it is signed, downtime is no longer only an engineering\nproblem; it accrues credits, triggers reporting obligations and, past a threshold, termination rights.\nThe person who owns delivery has to understand that commercial clock as well as the technical one.","how":"### Anatomy of a service SLA\n1. **Service definition.** Which endpoints, features and environments are covered. Production only;\n   beta features and sandboxes are explicitly out, *because* you need somewhere to ship experiments.\n2. **Metric definition.** The SLI formula in one sentence a query can implement: share of valid\n   requests returning a non-error response within the latency bound, or minutes in which a synthetic\n   probe failed. State the measurement point (your load balancer, not the customer's laptop) *because*\n   the two sides must compute the same number from data both can inspect. Partial outages need an\n   explicit rule, usually a \"bad minute\" defined as a minute whose error rate exceeds a threshold;\n   without it, a month of 3 percent errors is either invisible (probe minutes rarely fail) or a total\n   breach, depending on who is reading. Say also what counts as \"valid\": requests over the rate limit,\n   malformed requests and client cancellations are normally excluded from the denominator.\n3. **Window and target.** Almost always a calendar month, *because* credits are paid against monthly\n   invoices. The allowance is `allowed_downtime = (1 − target) × minutes_in_window`. For a 30-day month\n   (43,200 minutes): 99 percent allows 432 minutes, 99.5 allows 216, 99.9 allows 43.2, 99.95 allows\n   21.6 and 99.99 allows about 4.3. Read that table as a staffing statement: 43 minutes a month is\n   less than one slow page-acknowledge-diagnose-rollback cycle, so 99.9 percent implies automated\n   detection and rollback, not a human noticing.\n4. **Exclusions.** Scheduled maintenance with notice, customer-caused load beyond contracted limits,\n   force majeure, and features marked preview. Whether an upstream model provider's outage is excluded\n   is the most contested line in AI contracts; customers usually refuse, reasoning that your vendor\n   choice is your risk.\n5. **Remedies.** Tiered service credits as a percentage of the monthly fee, often stated as the sole\n   remedy, plus a termination right after repeated misses. Credits are usually claimed by the customer\n   within a stated period, not paid automatically.\n\n### Support SLAs\nSupport SLAs promise **response time**, the time until a qualified human acknowledges and starts work,\nper severity level (see incident-severity-and-escalation). Commit to response, and treat **resolution\ntime** as a target or a workaround commitment, *because* you control when someone picks up but not\nhow long an unknown root cause takes to find. Coverage hours matter as much as minutes: a 30-minute\nSev1 response around the clock implies a staffed on-call rotation, not one engineer with a phone.\n\n### Deriving what you can commit\nList the serial dependencies on the request path with their own SLAs, multiply them, then account for\nyour own failures: `ceiling = A_own × ∏ SLA_dependency`, and the ordering you want is\n`SLA < internal SLO < ceiling`. That product is your contractually backed ceiling, not a forecast: it assumes failures\nare independent and that each dependency runs exactly at its SLA floor (real availability may be better or,\nfor some providers, worse, so use measured history where you have it); set the internal SLO below it and the SLA below the SLO,\n*because* the gap is the room in which you detect and fix problems before they become contract events.\nRemember the asymmetry: when your provider misses its SLA you receive credits on your provider bill,\nwhile you owe credits on a customer contract that is usually far larger.\n\n### Operating the promise\nSigning is the start. The owner runs a month-to-date budget: minutes consumed so far against the\nallowance, visible to the on-call team, *because* a 20-minute incident on day 3 and on day 28 of a\nmonth that already burned 200 minutes are different decisions. When most of the budget is gone, the\nerror-budget policy should slow risky changes (prompt edits, model swaps, migrations) until the window\nresets. Each incident record gets an SLA-impact field (covered minutes, excluded or not, and why),\nfilled in during the postmortem rather than reconstructed at month end, *because* exclusion arguments\nare won with contemporaneous evidence. At month end, publish an attainment report built from the same\nquery the contract describes, and handle credit claims against it. Two commercial facts shape this:\ncredits are commonly capped and stated as the sole remedy, so their value to the customer is small\nnext to the business they lost; what customers actually escalate over is repeated misses, which is\nwhy termination-on-repeated-breach clauses are the ones that matter.\n\n### What AI changes\n- **Quality** rarely belongs in the SLA as a percentage. Put it in acceptance criteria, recurring eval\n  reports, and a commitment to remediate regressions within a set time.\n- **Model changes** need a notice clause: how far ahead you announce a model or prompt change that can\n  alter outputs, and whether the customer can pin a version.\n- **Latency** depends on output length, so define it as time to first token or per-request latency for\n  requests under a token cap; an unqualified p95 promise is broken by one long-answer workload.\n- **Throughput**: the SLA applies within contracted rate limits, *because* a 10x burst would otherwise\n  turn a provider quota into your breach.","example":"A worked month. A customer pays $40,000 per month (illustrative). The SLA promises 99.5 percent monthly\navailability, measured as minutes in which a synthetic probe against the production API fails twice in a\nrow. Credits: below 99.5 percent, 10 percent of the fee; below 99.0 percent, 25 percent; below 95 percent,\n50 percent. The month has 30 days, so 43,200 minutes and an allowance of 216 minutes.\n\nTwo incidents occur. On day 9 the model provider has a regional outage for 140 minutes and your fallback\nwas never configured for that region. The month-to-date budget now shows 76 minutes left for 21 days,\nwhich is the moment the error-budget policy should have frozen risky changes. It did not, and on day 22\na bad deploy breaks the API for 95 minutes. Total downtime is 235\nminutes; availability is 1 − 235/43,200 ≈ 99.456 percent. That is a breach in the first tier: a $4,000\ncredit if the customer files a claim. If the contract had excluded upstream provider outages, downtime\nwould be 95 minutes, 99.78 percent, no breach, which is exactly why that clause is fought over.\n\nNow the ceiling check behind the number. The provider's SLA is 99.9 percent, the vector database's\n99.95 percent, and your own service has historically run at about 99.9 percent. The serial product is\n0.999 × 0.9995 × 0.999 ≈ 0.9975, or about 99.75 percent if each runs at those levels and fails independently. A 99.5 percent SLA leaves a margin of about\na quarter of a percent; a 99.9 percent SLA, which sales had floated, would be breached in an ordinary\nmonth unless a tested cross-region or cross-provider fallback existed. Finally the asymmetry: your\nprovider bill is $6,000, so even a 10 percent provider credit returns $600 against the $4,000 you owe.","tradeoffs":["A tighter SLA wins enterprise deals and premium pricing, but each step multiplies the redundancy and on-call cost; it flips to worthwhile only when the price uplift exceeds that recurring cost.","Excluding upstream provider outages protects your margin, but customers read it as you not owning your architecture; it flips toward acceptable when the customer chose or mandated that provider.","Request-based measurement is fairer to partial outages than probe minutes, but needs logs both sides trust; it flips to probe-based measurement when the customer cannot see your logs or traffic is too sparse for per-minute error rates to mean anything.","Committing to resolution times reassures buyers, but you cannot control unknown root causes; it only becomes safe for narrow, well-understood failure classes with a documented workaround.","Automatic credits build trust and avoid claim disputes, but cost money on breaches nobody noticed; claim-based credits are cheaper but make every breach a negotiation."],"pitfalls":["Signing the number sales promised without computing the dependency ceiling; the symptom is breaching in a month where no single component missed its own SLA.","Defining the metric vaguely (\"the service is available\"); the symptom is a month-end dispute where your dashboard says 99.7 percent and the customer's monitoring says 98.9 percent.","Promising 24/7 response with no funded rotation; the symptom is a Sev1 acknowledged hours late because the only person on call was asleep or on a plane.","Treating the SLA target as the alerting threshold; the symptom is learning about a breach from the customer's credit claim instead of from an internal SLO alert days earlier.","Leaving the \"bad minute\" and valid-request rules undefined; the symptom is a month with a sustained partial error rate that your probe calls 100 percent available while the customer's users saw failures.","Changing the model or prompt without the notice the contract requires; the symptom is a customer escalating a \"silent regression\" that is also a contractual violation."],"when":"Write an explicit SLA for any production deployment a customer pays for and depends on, and for\ninternal platforms other teams build on. Skip formal credits for pilots and proofs of concept, but still\nwrite down response expectations so the pilot does not set an unspoken bar. Before signing, compute the\ndependency ceiling, confirm the on-call staffing behind every support commitment, and check that the\nmetric can be computed from data both sides can see. While operating, track SLA attainment monthly, alert\non the stricter internal SLO, and review the contract whenever architecture or providers change. In\ninterviews, expect \"how would you set the SLA for this system?\" and answer with the chain arithmetic.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Implements the SLI exactly as the contract defines it, builds the monthly attainment report, and wires internal SLO burn alerts that fire well before the contractual threshold.","architect":"Owns the gap between the dependency ceiling and the committed number, deciding which redundancy, fallback provider or region the promised SLA actually requires.","fde":"Negotiates exclusions, measurement points and model-change notice with the customer's procurement and operations teams, translating their fears into terms you can honestly meet."},"check":{"q":"A customer asks for 99.95 percent monthly availability and a two-hour resolution time for Sev1. Your\npath depends on one LLM provider (99.9 percent SLA) and a managed database (99.99 percent). What do you offer?","a":"First the ceiling: 0.999 × 0.9999 ≈ 0.9989 before any of my own failures, so 99.95 percent cannot\nbe backed on this architecture: the provider's own contract permits more downtime than my whole budget,\nand signing it means betting on the provider beating its SLA every month. I would offer 99.5 percent, or 99.9\npercent only if they fund a tested secondary provider and region, which raises the provider term close\nto one. On support, I commit to a Sev1 response time with 24/7 coverage that our rotation can staff,\nplus a workaround or status update cadence, but not a two-hour resolution, because root-cause time is\nunbounded. I would also add a model-change notice clause and define latency per request under a token cap."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why should the internal SLO be stricter than the external SLA?","options":["Because regulators require internal targets to exceed contractual ones","Because the gap gives time to detect and fix degradation before it becomes a contract breach","Because customers measure availability more leniently than providers do","Because service credits are calculated from the internal SLO"],"answer":1,"explanation":"If the alerting target equals the contract, you learn about problems only once you owe credits. A stricter SLO burns first and gives the team room to act; regulation and credit formulas do not require it."},{"q":"In a support SLA, which commitment is usually safe to make for every severity level?","options":["A guaranteed resolution time for each severity","A guaranteed root-cause report within one hour","A response time for a qualified human to acknowledge and begin work","A guarantee that no Sev1 incident will recur"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Response time is within your control if the rotation is staffed. Resolution depends on an unknown root cause, so promising it per severity converts hard incidents into automatic breaches."},{"q":"Your provider SLA is 99.9 percent and you sign 99.9 percent with your customer. What is the main flaw?","options":["Provider SLAs are measured quarterly, so the windows cannot match","Your service also depends on other components and your own code, so its ceiling is below 99.9 percent","Customer SLAs must always be at least one nine looser than provider SLAs","Credits from the provider will fully cover the credits you owe"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Serial dependencies multiply, so any additional failure source pushes you below the provider's number. There is no fixed one-nine rule, and provider credits are usually far smaller than what you owe."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/system-reliability","sources":["Beyer et al., 2016 — Site Reliability Engineering","Nygard, 2018 — Release It! (2nd ed.)"],"why":"A delivered AI system rarely fails the way a demo does. It fails at 3 a.m. when a provider rate-limits a\nregion, on the day a vendor silently updates a model, when one tenant's batch job starves everyone else,\nor when an agent's tenth tool call times out and leaves half a workflow done. None of these is caught by\n\"it works on my machine\", and most are not caught by unit tests, because they come from load, time,\ndependencies and change rather than from logic errors.\n\nThe naive answer is to treat reliability as a property you add once, typically by putting redundancy\neverywhere. That fails twice. First, it is expensive and still misses the dominant cause: postmortem\nexperience across the industry, Google's SRE writing included, finds that a large share of outages is\ntriggered by changes (deploys, config, prompts, model versions), and redundant copies running the same\nbad change fail together. Second, it optimises only one lever. A system\nthat fails rarely but takes four hours to diagnose each time can be less available than one that fails\nweekly and rolls back in five minutes.\n\nSomeone has to own the whole picture: which failures matter, which lever is cheapest to pull next, and\nwhether the system is getting better or worse. Without that owner, reliability work happens only after\nincidents and only on whatever the last incident touched.","how":"### The four levers\nAvailability over time is approximately MTBF / (MTBF + MTTR): mean uptime between failures over the whole\ncycle of working plus being broken. (Strictly that numerator is MTTF, mean time to failure; if your MTBF\nalready includes repair time, the formula is (MTBF − MTTR) / MTBF. Conventions differ, so say which you use.) Split MTTR into time to detect, time to diagnose and time to\nrecover, and add blast radius (the share of users affected), and you have every lever you can pull.\nThey combine into one cost function the owner can rank work by:\n\n```\nuser_impact_minutes per period ≈ Σ over incidents of\n    (time_to_detect + time_to_diagnose + time_to_recover) × blast_radius_share\nexpected for a failure mode ≈ frequency × mean_duration × mean_blast_radius\n```\n\nEach term is a separate lever, and the product form is the point: halving any one term halves the\nimpact, so the cheapest term to halve is where the next sprint goes.\n\n\n1. **Fail less often (raise MTBF).** Change safety beats hardware: canary and shadow releases, evaluation\n   gates on prompt and model changes, pinned model versions, config review. *Because* change causes most\n   incidents, this is usually where the first effort pays off.\n2. **Fail smaller (reduce blast radius).** Per-tenant quotas, bulkheads between workloads, feature flags,\n   cell or region isolation. A failure that hits 2 percent of users for an hour costs far less error\n   budget than one that hits everyone. Two further tools shrink the *depth* of a failure rather than its\n   width. Graceful degradation serves a worse answer instead of none (a smaller or secondary model, a\n   cached response, retrieval-only results without generation, a read-only mode), *because* users\n   tolerate degraded far better than down. Timeouts, retry budgets, circuit breakers and load shedding\n   stop one slow dependency from exhausting threads, connections or quota for everything else,\n   *because* the usual way a small failure becomes a big one is queueing behind it.\n3. **Notice sooner (reduce detection time).** Alert on symptoms users feel (SLO burn, failed tasks,\n   quality-eval pass rate on sampled traffic), not on CPU. For AI systems, quality monitoring is the\n   difference between detecting a regression in an hour or from a customer email a week later.\n4. **Recover faster (reduce diagnose and recover time).** One-step rollback for code, prompts and model\n   versions; runbooks; traces that connect a user's request to every model and tool call; automatic\n   failover for failures that need sub-minute recovery, *because* humans cannot act that fast.\n\n### Composition arithmetic\nSerial dependencies multiply: a request needing four components at 99.99, 99.9, 99.95 and 99.9 percent\nsucceeds about 99.74 percent of the time. Redundant alternatives combine as 1 − ∏(1 − aᵢ), but only if\nfailures are independent, which shared regions, shared quotas and shared bad configs violate. Multi-step\nagents compound the same way per task: ten steps at 99 percent each complete about 90 percent of tasks.\n\n### AI-specific failure classes\n- **Dependency failures**: provider outages, rate limits (HTTP 429), regional quota exhaustion.\n- **Silent quality failures**: a model update, a retrieval index gone stale, a prompt edit; every request\n  returns 200 and the answers get worse.\n- **Partial side effects**: an agent fails mid-workflow; reliability then requires idempotent tools and\n  resumable state, not just retries.\n- **Nondeterminism**: the same input sometimes fails, so \"could not reproduce\" is not evidence of a fix.\n\n### The ownership loop\n1. **Define SLOs**, including a quality SLO (for example, the pass rate of an automated evaluation on\n   sampled production traffic), *because* availability alone cannot see the silent failures above.\n2. **Agree an error-budget policy in advance**: what happens when the budget is spent (change freeze\n   except fixes, reliability work moves to the top of the queue). Agreeing it before the crisis is what\n   stops the argument from being re-litigated every time a launch is due.\n3. **Review weekly**: budget burn, incidents, and the per-incident split of detect, diagnose and recover\n   time, *because* incident count alone hides which lever is failing.\n4. **Rank failure modes** from incidents and failure analysis with the impact formula, and fund the\n   top items, not the most recent ones.\n5. **Verify** with game days, failover drills and chaos tests, *because* a recovery path that has not\n   been exercised should be assumed broken. Then repeat.\n\nBefore launch, a production readiness review checks each lever explicitly.","example":"The arithmetic an owner runs before deciding where the next sprint of reliability work goes. All\ncomponent figures are illustrative, not vendor numbers.\n\n```python\nfrom math import prod\n\ndef serial(*a):   return prod(a)                        # every component must work\ndef parallel(*a): return 1 - prod(1 - x for x in a)     # any one suffices; assumes independence\ndef availability(mtbf_h, mttr_h): return mtbf_h / (mtbf_h + mttr_h)\n\ngateway, llm, vectors, app = 0.9999, 0.999, 0.9995, 0.999\nbackup_llm = 0.995\nprint(f\"single provider: {serial(gateway, llm, vectors, app):.2%}\")                      # 99.74%\nprint(f\"with fallback:   {serial(gateway, parallel(llm, backup_llm), vectors, app):.2%}\") # 99.84%\n\n# The app's own term: one incident per month (~720 h) at two recovery speeds\nfor mttr in (2.0, 0.25):\n    print(f\"MTTR {mttr:>4} h -> {availability(720, mttr):.3%}\")   # 99.723% vs 99.965%\n\n# Agent task of 10 tool calls, 1% transient failure each, without and with one retry\np, n = 0.01, 10\nprint(f\"no retry {(1 - p) ** n:.1%}, one retry {(1 - p ** 2) ** n:.2%}\")  # 90.4% vs 99.90%\n```\n\nThree conclusions follow. The fallback provider buys only a tenth of a percent, because the other\nserial terms now dominate. Cutting recovery from two hours to fifteen minutes (one-step rollback plus a\nrunbook) moves the app's own term far more. And retries rescue the agent only if failures are transient\nand the tools are idempotent; a deterministic bug fails both attempts.","tradeoffs":["Redundancy raises availability for independent failures, but doubles cost and adds failover paths that rot unless exercised; it flips to worthwhile once recovery time, not failure rate, is already small.","Strict change gates (evals, canaries, approvals) cut change-induced incidents, but slow delivery; loosen them when the error budget is healthy and tighten them when it is being burned.","Quality monitoring on sampled traffic catches silent regressions, but costs evaluation spend and judge noise; it flips to essential as soon as outputs feed decisions without human review.","Automatic failover recovers in seconds, but a flapping health check can cause the outage it guards against; prefer manual failover where minutes of recovery are contractually acceptable."],"pitfalls":["Measuring reliability only as HTTP success; the symptom is green dashboards during a week of customer complaints about wrong answers after a provider's model update.","Adding retries everywhere without idempotency or budgets; the symptom is duplicate side effects and retry storms that turn a brief provider blip into a long outage.","Building a fallback or degraded path nobody tests; the symptom is discovering during the first real outage that the backup provider's credentials expired, its prompt format was never adapted, or its quota cannot absorb full production traffic.","Fixing whatever the last incident touched; the symptom is the same failure class recurring across postmortems while the team feels busy.","Assuming failures are independent when computing redundancy; the symptom is both \"redundant\" providers failing together because they share a region, quota or upstream."],"when":"Reliability ownership starts the day a system has users who depend on it, not after the first outage.\nBefore launch, run a readiness review against the four levers: how changes are gated, how failures are\ncontained, which symptom alerts exist, and how long rollback takes. In operation, let the error budget and\nthe ranked failure list decide what to fund, and measure MTTR and detection time per incident, not just\nincident count. Do not gold-plate a pilot with multi-region redundancy; do insist even there on rollback\nand quality monitoring. In interviews, reliability questions reward reasoning about which lever is\ncheapest for the given failure profile, with the composition arithmetic shown.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the mechanisms that move each lever: idempotent tools, retry budgets, one-step rollback for prompts and models, and traces that make a failed task diagnosable.","architect":"Decides where redundancy, isolation and fallback belong by running the composition arithmetic, and sets the reliability targets each component must meet.","fde":"Learns how the customer's network, identity provider and data systems fail, because in their environment those dependencies, not your code, often dominate the reliability budget."},"check":{"q":"Your assistant had six incidents last quarter: two provider outages of 30 minutes, three bad prompt\ndeploys each taking about three hours to notice and roll back, and one stale index. Where do you invest first?","a":"The prompt deploys dominate: roughly nine hours of degraded service versus one hour from the\nprovider, and they are self-inflicted, so they are cheap to fix. The formula also says where in their\nduration to cut: I would expect most of those three hours to be detection, because a bad prompt returns 200s, so I would confirm that from the incident timelines before choosing the fix. I would add an evaluation gate and a\ncanary for prompt changes to cut their frequency, quality monitoring on sampled traffic to detect a bad\nprompt within minutes, and one-step prompt rollback to shrink recovery. The stale index points to the\nsame detection gap and needs a freshness check. A second provider is worth less until those are done,\nbecause it addresses the smallest slice of downtime."},"quiz":[{"q":"Which change most directly reduces MTTR rather than failure frequency?","options":["Adding an evaluation gate before prompt changes reach production","Making prompt and model versions revertible in one step","Pinning the model version instead of using a floating alias","Running a canary on 5 percent of traffic before full rollout"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Recovery time shrinks when reverting is fast and safe. Gates, pinning and canaries prevent or contain bad changes, which raises MTBF or reduces blast radius rather than shortening recovery."},{"q":"Two redundant LLM providers each claim 99.9 percent. Why might the pair deliver far less than 1 − 0.001²?","options":["Because SLA percentages cannot be multiplied","Because the formula applies only to hardware failures","Because failures can be correlated through shared regions, quotas, client code or configuration","Because failover always takes longer than the outage"],"answer":2,"explanation":"The parallel formula assumes independence. A shared cloud region, a common client library bug or a bad shared config takes both paths down at once, so real gains are smaller."},{"q":"Why is answer quality part of system reliability for an LLM product?","options":["Because quality metrics are required in every SLA","Because a system can return successful responses while users receive wrong answers, which is a failure to them","Because low quality always causes HTTP errors eventually","Because quality monitoring replaces availability monitoring"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Reliability means doing the right thing for users. Silent quality regressions produce 200 responses, so they need their own SLO and monitoring alongside availability, not instead of it."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/technical-documentation","sources":["Official docs: Diátaxis documentation framework","Nygard, 2011 — Documenting Architecture Decisions","Mitchell et al., 2019 — Model Cards for Model Reporting"],"why":"Delivered systems outlive the attention of the people who built them. The forward-deployed engineer\nrotates to the next customer, the original architect changes teams, and the customer's operations team\ninherits a pipeline at 2 a.m. with an error message they have never seen. Every question that is not\nanswered in writing becomes a page to someone who has moved on, or a guess. Security reviewers,\nauditors and new team members ask the same questions again and again; without documents, senior people\nanswer them in meetings forever.\n\nThe naive approaches fail. \"The code is the documentation\" answers what the system does but never why it\ndoes it that way, what was rejected, or what to do when it breaks. One giant wiki page that mixes a\ntutorial, a configuration reference and design rationale serves nobody: the newcomer drowns in\nreference tables and the on-call engineer cannot find the one command they need. And documentation\nwritten once at the end of a project decays immediately, because the system keeps changing and nothing\nforces the docs to follow. The cause is structural, not laziness: the author pays the cost of updating a\ndocument and a different person, later, pays the cost of it being wrong, so without a mechanism that\nputs the update at the moment of change, drift is the default. A stale doc is worse than none: it is\ntrusted, and it is wrong.\n\nAI systems sharpen this. Behaviour depends on artefacts that are not obviously code: the model version,\nthe prompt, the retrieval index, the evaluation set. When output changes, the first question is which of\nthese changed, and only documentation and registries can answer it.","how":"### One reader, one job per document\nThe Diátaxis framework separates documentation into four types *because* readers arrive in four\ndifferent modes, and mixing them makes each worse:\n1. **Tutorials**: learning by doing, for a newcomer. A guaranteed-to-work path from nothing to a first\n   result. No options, no edge cases.\n2. **How-to guides**: a competent reader with a goal. \"Rotate the provider API key\", \"add a new tenant\".\n   Steps, preconditions, how to verify success.\n3. **Reference**: lookup. Configuration keys, API endpoints, environment variables, error codes.\n   Generated from the source of truth wherever possible (OpenAPI, config schemas) *because* hand-written\n   reference drifts first.\n4. **Explanation**: understanding. Architecture, design rationale, trade-offs and constraints.\n\n### The delivery document set\nFor a system handed to a customer or another team, the minimum set usually maps onto those types:\n- **Architecture overview** (explanation): components, data flows, trust boundaries, external\n  dependencies. Security review starts here, so draw the data flow, not just the boxes.\n- **Architecture decision records** (explanation): one short record per significant decision with\n  context, options and consequences, *because* six months later \"why not just use X?\" is the question.\n- **Runbooks** (how-to): one per alert, in a fixed order: symptom and how to confirm it, user impact,\n  the first safe action (usually rollback or failover, *because* it stabilises before anyone\n  understands the cause), diagnosis steps with expected output, and a \"stop and escalate if\" line\n  naming who to call.\n- **API and configuration reference**, generated where possible.\n- **AI system card** (explanation plus reference): model and version, prompt versions, retrieval sources,\n  evaluation sets and latest results, intended use, out-of-scope uses and known failure modes. This\n  borrows the model-card idea and extends it to the whole system.\n- **Operations and support guide**: who owns what, escalation paths, SLAs, how to roll back.\n\n### Docs as code\nKeep documents in the repository, change them in the same pull request as the code, and review them with\nit. *Because* review is where staleness is cheapest to catch, the pull-request template should ask\n\"which docs does this change invalidate?\". Add CI checks: broken links, executable code samples,\ngenerated reference rebuilt on each build, and freshness rules mapping docs to the code they cover. Give\nevery document an owner and a review date; an unowned document is a stale document waiting to happen.\n\n### Versioned with what is deployed\nDocumentation must describe the version the reader is running. If a customer runs release 2.3 and the\ndocs describe 2.5, the runbook's commands may not exist yet. Tag docs with the release that built them,\npublish per-version reference, and put the deployed model, prompt and index versions in the system card\nby pulling them from the registry or deployment manifest rather than typing them, *because* typed\nversion strings are the first thing to go stale.\n\n### Testing documentation\nA document is only verified when someone other than its author uses it to do the job. Have a new team\nmember follow the tutorial from a clean machine and record each place they stalled; run a tabletop\nincident where responders may use only the runbooks; ask the customer's operator to perform a rollback\nfrom the operations guide before handoff. Each stall is a bug in the doc. This is the documentation\nequivalent of a failover drill, and for the same reason: an unexercised path should be presumed broken.\n\n### Writing so it gets used\nLead with the answer, then the detail. Make commands copy-pasteable and state what success looks like.\nWrite for the reader's state: an on-call engineer at 2 a.m. needs the first safe action, not the history.","example":"A CI check that fails the build when code changes but the document that describes it does not. Each doc\ndeclares which paths it covers in a `covers:` line; the check compares last-commit times.\n\n```python\n\"\"\"Fail CI when documented code changed after its doc was last updated.\"\"\"\nimport pathlib, re, subprocess, sys\n\ndef last_commit_ts(path: str) -> int:\n    out = subprocess.run([\"git\", \"log\", \"-1\", \"--format=%ct\", \"--\", path],\n                         capture_output=True, text=True, check=True).stdout.strip()\n    return int(out or 0)                     # 0 if the path has no history\n\nstale = []\nfor doc in pathlib.Path(\"docs\").rglob(\"*.md\"):\n    m = re.search(r\"^covers:\\s*(.+)$\", doc.read_text(), re.M)\n    if not m:                                # e.g. covers: src/extract/ prompts/invoice.yaml\n        continue\n    doc_ts = last_commit_ts(str(doc))\n    for target in m.group(1).split():\n        if last_commit_ts(target) > doc_ts:\n            stale.append(f\"{doc}: '{target}' changed after this doc was last updated\")\n\nprint(\"\\n\".join(stale) or \"all covered docs are fresh\")\nsys.exit(1 if stale else 0)\n```\n\nOn a customer deployment, `docs/runbooks/extraction-backlog.md` might cover `src/extract/` and\n`prompts/invoice.yaml`. When someone edits the invoice prompt, CI fails until the runbook is touched,\nwhich forces a reviewer to ask whether its steps and expected outputs still hold. The check is a nudge,\nnot proof: a trivial edit silences it, so pair it with the pull-request question about invalidated docs.\nIt also needs real history: many CI systems check out a shallow clone of depth one by default, where every\npath reports the same last commit and the check silently passes, so fetch full history (or at least the\nmerge base) in the job that runs it.","tradeoffs":["Docs as code keeps documentation current with changes, but non-engineers find repositories hard to edit; it flips toward a wiki for customer-facing guides owned by support or product staff.","Generated reference never drifts from the source, but reads poorly and lacks intent; it flips to hand-written sections where the reader needs context a schema cannot express.","A full document set eases handoff and audits, but every page is a maintenance liability; prefer fewer, owned documents when the system is still changing weekly.","Detailed runbooks shorten recovery for known failures, but encourage blind command-following; they need a \"stop and escalate if\" line for situations outside what the author anticipated."],"pitfalls":["Mixing tutorial, reference and rationale in one page; the symptom is readers asking questions in chat that the document technically answers somewhere in its fourth screen.","Writing documentation once at handoff; the symptom is the customer's team discovering, during their first incident, that the runbook names a config key that was renamed months ago.","Documenting what the code does but not why; the symptom is a new owner \"simplifying\" a retry or a prompt instruction that existed to work around a specific provider failure.","Omitting model and prompt versions from system documentation; the symptom is a behaviour change nobody can attribute because no one knows what was running last month.","Only ever reviewing docs by reading them; the symptom is the first real use revealing that a runbook assumes access, tools or tribal context the customer's operator does not have.","Leaving documents without owners; the symptom is a wiki where every page has a \"this may be out of date\" banner and nobody trusts any of it."],"when":"Write documentation whenever someone other than the current author must operate, review, extend or\napprove the system, which for delivered work is always. Start early with the architecture overview and\ndecision records, because security reviews and design debates need them; write runbooks with the alerts\nthey serve; write the handoff and system card before the people who know the answers leave. Do not write\nlong documents for throwaway prototypes. Measure usefulness by the questions that stop arriving and by\nincident timelines: how long did responders spend finding what to do? In interviews, describing how you\nkept documentation true, not merely that you wrote it, signals real ownership experience.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Updates docs in the same pull request as the change, keeps reference generated from schemas, and writes runbooks that start with the first safe action.","architect":"Owns the architecture overview and decision records, and sets the documentation standard every delivered system must meet before it is considered production-ready.","fde":"Writes the handoff set the customer's operators will actually use, and the data-flow and system card pages their security and compliance reviewers ask for first."},"check":{"q":"You are handing an AI document-extraction system to a customer's operations team in three weeks. What\ndocumentation do you produce, in what order, and how do you keep it accurate after you leave?","a":"First the architecture overview with the data flow and trust boundaries, because their security\nreview gates go-live. Then runbooks for each alert, written from real incidents in the pilot, then the\noperations guide with ownership, escalation and rollback. Next an AI system card listing model and\nprompt versions, evaluation results, intended use and known failure modes, plus generated configuration\nreference. Decision records for the major choices go in alongside. To keep it true, it lives in their\nrepository, each doc has a named owner on their side, the pull-request template asks which docs a change\ninvalidates, and CI checks links and freshness. I would run one tabletop incident using only the docs."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why separate how-to guides from tutorials instead of writing one combined guide?","options":["Tutorials must be generated automatically while how-to guides are written by hand","A newcomer needs a single guaranteed path to learn, while a competent reader needs steps for a specific goal","How-to guides are only for internal staff and tutorials only for customers","Combined guides cannot include code samples"],"answer":1,"explanation":"Readers arrive in different modes. A tutorial removes choices to build confidence; a how-to assumes competence and targets a goal. Mixing them gives the learner too many options and the expert too much hand-holding."},{"q":"Which artefact best answers \"why did we choose this retrieval design over fine-tuning?\" a year later?","options":["The API reference generated from the OpenAPI schema","The runbook for the retrieval latency alert","An architecture decision record capturing context, options considered and consequences","The commit message of the first retrieval commit"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Decision records exist to preserve rationale and rejected alternatives. Reference and runbooks describe what and how, and commit messages are scattered and rarely capture the options weighed."},{"q":"What is the strongest reason to keep documentation in the same repository as the code?","options":["Repositories render Markdown better than wikis do","It removes the need for document owners","Docs can be changed and reviewed in the same pull request that changes the behaviour they describe","It makes the documentation publicly visible"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Staleness is cheapest to catch at the moment of change. Co-locating docs lets review and CI tie them to the code change; ownership is still needed, and visibility depends on repository settings."}]},{"key":"delivery-ownership/productizing-repeated-customer-solutions","sources":[],"why":"A forward-deployed engineer is paid to make one customer successful fast, so the first build is bespoke:\ntheir connector, their prompt, their output format. The second customer asks for \"the same thing\", and\nthe fastest path is to copy the repository and change what differs. By the fifth customer there are five\nforks. A security fix has to be applied five times, and a model upgrade means re-tuning and re-evaluating\nfive prompts that have drifted apart. Every improvement made for one customer is unavailable to the\nothers, and the engineers who know each fork become permanent bottlenecks. Delivery cost per customer\nstops falling, which is the economic signature of a services business, not a product.\n\nThe naive opposite fails too. Building a general platform after the first customer means guessing which\nparts will vary, and with one example you cannot tell an essential requirement from an accident of that\ncustomer's environment. The result is an abstraction with knobs nobody uses and no seam where the second\ncustomer actually differs.\n\nProductizing exists to find the moment in between: enough repetition to see the real shape, early\nenough that the forks have not diverged beyond recovery. It is also a feedback channel. The field sees\ndemand before the product team does, and productizing is how that evidence becomes roadmap.","how":"### 1. Detect repetition\nKeep an inventory of every deployment and what each component does. A common heuristic is the rule of\nthree: build the first, copy the second while recording every difference, extract on the third,\n*because* with two instances you cannot tell which of their differences will recur, while a third\nusually shows what genuinely varies and what was coincidence. Three is a heuristic, not a law: a\nwell-understood domain can justify extracting at two, and a very heterogeneous one may need more.\n\nCount alone does not decide it; the economics do. Each recurring change (model upgrade, security fix,\nshared bug fix) costs roughly `N × c_fork` across N forks and `c_core + N × c_tenant` on a product,\nwhere `c_tenant` is running and reviewing one customer's regression suite. Extraction pays back when\nthe per-change saving, `N × (c_fork − c_tenant) − c_core`, summed over the changes you expect in your\nplanning horizon, exceeds the one-time cost of building the core and migrating the forks. The formula\nmakes two things visible: the saving grows linearly with customer count, and it is negative for small N\nif the core is expensive to change, which is the quantitative form of \"too early\".\n\n### 2. Classify every difference\nDiff the instances component by component and put each difference in one bucket:\n- **Configuration**: differences in data, not logic. Field lists, thresholds, clause taxonomies, prompt\n  variables, few-shot examples, output destinations. These become a validated, versioned config schema.\n- **Extension**: customer-specific logic that cannot be data, such as a connector to a proprietary system.\n  These go behind a defined interface, *because* an interface lets the core evolve without breaking them.\n- **Accident**: differences with no reason beyond who wrote it and when. Pick one behaviour and converge.\n- **Genuine one-off**: a requirement no other customer shares. It stays outside the product as paid\n  services work, *because* pulling it into the core taxes every other customer.\n\n### 3. Build the core and its seams\nThe core owns everything shared: orchestration, the prompt template, model routing and version pinning,\nevaluation harness, observability, security controls. Prompts become one template with slots rather than\nN forked prompts, so a model upgrade is tuned once. Evaluation splits into a shared core suite plus a\nper-customer regression suite, *because* the core must prove it did not break anyone in particular.\n\nConfiguration is now code that ships. A tenant's config is validated against the schema on load (bad\nconfig fails at deploy, not at 2 a.m. on the first document), versioned, reviewed, and gated by that\ntenant's regression suite, *because* changing a clause description changes prompt text, and prompt text\nchanges behaviour as surely as a code change does.\n\n### 4. Migrate the forks\nMove existing customers onto the product, least-divergent first, each gated by their regression suite\nshowing parity with the fork's behaviour. Stopping halfway is the worst state: you now maintain a product\nand forks. Set a date after which forks receive security fixes only.\n\n### 5. Transfer ownership\nA product team owns the core's roadmap, versioning and deprecation, including an upgrade policy: every\ntenant moves to a new core release within a stated window, *because* letting each customer pin a\nversion indefinitely recreates the forks in time instead of in code. Field engineers\nconsume the core, contribute extensions, and file evidence-backed requests. Track time to onboard a new customer, the share of each\ndeployment that is configuration rather than custom code, and engineering hours per customer per month.","example":"Three customers run contract-review pipelines built by the field team. Customer A pulls from SharePoint\nand writes to Salesforce, B from Box into Jira, C from S3 into a webhook. Diffing the forks shows the\ningestion connectors and output sinks are extensions, the clause types, risk threshold and few-shot\nexamples are configuration, two different chunking strategies are an accident, and C's requirement to\nscore clauses against its internal playbook is a genuine one-off. The resulting customer config:\n\n```python\nfrom typing import Literal, Protocol\nfrom pydantic import BaseModel, Field\n\nclass Connector(Protocol):                 # extension point: custom code lives behind this\n    def fetch(self, since: str) -> list[bytes]: ...\n\nclass ClauseType(BaseModel):\n    name: str\n    description: str                       # injected into the shared extraction prompt template\n    examples: list[str] = Field(default_factory=list, max_length=5)\n\nclass CustomerConfig(BaseModel):           # configuration: data, validated, versioned per tenant\n    schema_version: Literal[2]\n    tenant_id: str\n    source: Literal[\"sharepoint\", \"box\", \"s3\", \"custom\"]\n    clause_types: list[ClauseType]\n    risk_threshold: float = Field(0.7, ge=0.0, le=1.0)\n    sink: Literal[\"salesforce\", \"jira\", \"webhook\"]\n    model_alias: str = \"contract-extraction-default\"   # pinned and upgraded centrally\n```\n\nThe economics, with illustrative effort figures: if a model upgrade costs about 40 engineer-hours per\nfork to re-tune and re-evaluate, three forks cost 120 hours and ten cost 400. On the product, the same\nupgrade might cost 50 hours on the core plus 4 hours per customer to run and review its regression suite:\n62 hours at three customers, 90 at ten. The per-upgrade saving is 58 hours at three customers and 310\nat ten. If building the core and migrating the forks costs 600 hours (also illustrative), upgrades alone\ntake about ten cycles to pay it back at three customers and two at ten; security and bug fixes shorten\nboth, which is why the honest case is made with all recurring changes, not one. The fourth customer then\nneeds a connector and a config file, not a new codebase.","tradeoffs":["Productizing early avoids fork divergence, but with fewer than three examples you will likely abstract the wrong seam; it flips toward early extraction only when the domain is already well understood.","A rich configuration schema lets customers onboard without code, but every option multiplies the test matrix; prefer an extension point once options start interacting with each other.","Migrating every fork onto the core cuts long-term maintenance, but costs revalidation effort and customer goodwill now; leave a fork in place only if that customer is winding down.","Refusing one-offs keeps the core clean, but can lose a strategic deal; accept them as services work outside the core, priced to cover their ongoing maintenance."],"pitfalls":["Abstracting from a single deployment; the symptom is the second customer needing changes inside the \"generic\" core rather than in its configuration.","Letting configuration become a programming language; the symptom is config files with conditionals and expressions that only the original engineer can safely edit.","Stopping the migration halfway; the symptom is the team maintaining the product plus most of the old forks, with fixes landing in one and not the others.","Forking the shared prompt per customer \"just for now\"; the symptom is a model upgrade that passes core evals but regresses two customers whose prompt copies nobody re-tested.","Letting tenants pin core versions with no upgrade window; the symptom is production running several core releases at once, with each fix backported by hand, which is the fork problem again.","Productizing without per-customer regression suites; the symptom is a core release that silently breaks one customer's edge cases, discovered from their support ticket."],"when":"Start productizing when the same solution shape appears for the third customer, when onboarding time per\ncustomer has stopped falling, or when a model upgrade or security fix has to be applied in several places.\nHold off while the domain is still being discovered and each customer teaches you something new about the\nshape. Before committing, run the break-even arithmetic with your own hour estimates for recurring\nchanges and for the extraction itself. Measure onboarding time, custom code per deployment and maintenance hours per customer before and\nafter. In day-to-day field work, the habit that makes this possible is recording every difference you\nintroduce when copying a solution. In interviews, describe how you decided what became configuration,\nwhat became an extension, and what you refused.","role_lens":{"engineer":"Builds the config schema, the extension interfaces and the per-customer regression suites that prove each migration preserved the behaviour the customer relied on.","architect":"Decides where the seams go, which differences become configuration versus extension, and when a one-off is refused from the core to protect every other customer.","fde":"Spots the repetition first, documents how each deployment diverges, and brings the evidence to the product team instead of quietly maintaining another fork."},"check":{"q":"Four customers run forks of your support-triage agent. One adds a custom escalation rule engine; the\nother three differ only in categories, tone and destination queue. A new customer signs next month. What do you do?","a":"The three similar forks show the shape: categories, tone guidance and queue mapping become a\nvalidated config schema, the prompt becomes one template with slots, and destinations become sink\nextensions behind an interface. I would build the core with a shared eval suite, write regression suites\nfrom each customer's real tickets, and migrate the least-divergent fork first. The rule engine is either an\nextension point, if others are likely to need rules, or a supported one-off outside the core. The new\ncustomer onboards onto the product, not a fifth fork, which is the first proof the extraction worked."},"quiz":[{"q":"Why is the third instance, rather than the first, the usual point to extract a product?","options":["Because contracts typically forbid reusing code before three customers","Because several instances are needed to tell essential variation from accidents of one environment","Because model providers discount usage after three tenants","Because the first two builds are always discarded"],"answer":1,"explanation":"With one example every detail looks essential. Comparing three shows which differences recur and belong in configuration and which were incidental, so the abstraction fits real demand."},{"q":"A customer needs a connector to a proprietary document system nobody else uses. Where should it live?","options":["Inside the core pipeline, behind a customer-ID conditional","In a new fork of the whole product for that customer","Behind a defined extension interface, implemented outside the core","As new keys in the shared configuration schema"],"answer":2,"explanation":"Logic that cannot be expressed as data belongs behind an interface so the core can evolve without it. Customer-ID conditionals and forks recreate exactly the divergence productizing removes."},{"q":"What best indicates productizing is working?","options":["The product exposes more configuration options than any single fork had","Every customer request is accepted into the core","The field team stops talking to customers about requirements","Onboarding time and per-customer maintenance hours fall as the customer count grows"],"answer":3,"explanation":"The point is that delivery cost per customer declines. More options or accepting everything usually raises cost, and field feedback is the input productizing depends on."}]}]});
